  "explanation": {
    "summary": "Per decade: how much warmer the planet was (NASA), and the share of the US with extreme heat, extreme cold and heavy downpours in an average year (NOAA). Extreme heat went from 2% of the US in the 1910s to 48% in the 2020s.",
    "methodology": "NASA GISTEMP v4 annual global temperature, averaged per decade. NOAA Climate Extremes Index for the contiguous US: step 1 (daily high temperatures much above or much below normal) and step 4 (share of the US with a much greater than normal share of rain from extreme 1-day events). 'Much above' or 'much below' means in the top or bottom 10% on record for that place. NOAA's single total index is not used, because it adds hot and cold extremes together. The last decade has 6 years so far.",
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "date": "1930-01-01",
        "title": "The Dust Bowl decade",
        "description": "In the 1930s, 17% of the US had extreme heat in an average year, before modern warming.",
